  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Strong high pressure building into the Mid-Atlantic \nand low pressure well off the FL coast will result in a period \nof continued large waves across the OBX. The high waves combined\nwith King Tides this week will lead to additional minor coastal\nflooding impacts.\n\nFor Hatteras Island, seas offshore continue in the 7-9 foot\nrange and will remain in this range today, and then slowly \nsubside through the coming week, but long period swells will\ncontinue to cause issues for vulnerable areas. For ocean-side: \nhigh tide is between 0730-0800 EDT today. Soundside high tides \nlag from 2 to 3 hours behind the oceanside tides. The National \nParks Service and NCDOT have reported substantial beach erosion \nfrom south Nags Head to Buxton, along with significant overwash \noccurring on side streets in Rodanthe. Due to hazardous road \nconditions and flooding, NC-12 remains closed from the Marc \nBasnight Bridge to Rodanthe. \n\nCoastal flooding will be likely well into this week despite the\ndiminishing of winds, with \"sunny day\" flooding across much of \nthe tidal regions of ENC around times of high tide, then slowly\nimprove as the King Tides ease later in the week and into the\nweekend. Coastal flood advisories continue through the high tide\ncycle on Thursday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Pleasantly cooler and drier air mass will \nremain with us through early this week, with near climo highs, \nto slightly below avg lows, esp Sun and Mon nights when good \nradiational cooling yet again sets up with lows well down into \nthe 40s with some 30s again possible TUE morning when the high \npressure ridges into ENC allowing for excellent rad cooling\nunder clear skies, calm winds, and low TD's. Hts/thicknesses \nthen begin to build by mid week, with temps climbing back above \nclimo with highs in the mid 70s, then upper 70s by Thursday into\nthe weekend. Lows will remain closer to climo (50s) as dry \nairmass remains with a large diurnal temp range.",
